Title: SWEET JESUS
Credit: A screenplay
Draft: Storyboard draft

FADE IN:

INT. SUNNY'S WASH-N-DRY - FULTON STREET - THURSDAY AFTERNOON

Fluorescent hum. Six washers. Five behaving. [[CC: Fluorescent lights hum.]]

The sixth goes CLANK. CLANK. CLANK. [[CC: A washer clanks in rhythm.]]

BUG RAY, 15, holds a microphone to the machine like he's interviewing it. Beside him, a shoebox cassette recorder, needle jumping.

PEANUT RAY, 19, folds towels at the table. Handsome in a way that worries mothers. He tries not to laugh at his brother.

@BUG
It's a beat. Hold still, you're bleeding into my take.

@PEANUT
(not moving)
That machine's been eating quarters since I was seven.

@BUG
It's got rhythm.

@PEANUT
So does my elbow. You wanna put that on the radio?

On the folding table, a left-behind Daily News: FORD TO CITY: DROP DEAD. Peanut reads it standing up.

@PEANUT
President told the whole city to drop dead.

@BUG
So?

@PEANUT
So everybody's quitting at once.

He slaps two quarters on the machine.

@PEANUT
Buy your rhythm.

He pushes out the propped door. Sunlight takes him. [[CC: The door bangs against its stop.]]

Bug hits RECORD, sets the mic down, and wanders to the back with his headphones on. The recorder runs. Nobody listening. [[CC: The recorder clicks; the washer clanks.]]

Through the open door, under the dryers:

@MAN (O.S.)
This one's on the house.

@PEANUT (O.S.)
What, you hit the lottery?

@MAN (O.S.)
Don't push it.

Peanut LAUGHS -- big, dumb, nineteen forever. Out of focus on the step: a cigarette cherry, a gold pinky ring catching sun. Nothing else. [[CC: Peanut laughs.]]

Bug comes back, rewinds, ejects. In ballpoint he labels the cassette DRYER BEAT. [[CC: Tape rewinds and clicks off.]]

INT. RAY APARTMENT - BED-STUY - FRIDAY MORNING - HALLOWEEN

No music. ETTA RAY, 52, sits with coffee she isn't drinking. The front door is open. Neighbors are being careful on the stairs.

Two officers wait for her signature. A covered stretcher has already gone downstairs. Her hand is perfectly steady until the pen runs out.

@OFFICER
We'll need the medical examiner to make it official.

@ETTA
You already told the people downstairs what it was.

@OFFICER
I said it looks like an overdose.

@ETTA
You said that's what boys like him do. I heard you through my door.

The officer looks at the form. Etta signs with the dead pen anyway, pressing hard enough to leave her name in the paper.

In the bedroom doorway, Bug holds the recorder, thumb over PLAY. He doesn't press it.

FREEZE on the thumb. TITLE: SWEET JESUS.

EXT. RAY APARTMENT - FRIDAY MORNING

A Halloween mask hangs from a fire escape. JACK MONROE, 43, waits on the stoop with a paper twist of sunflower seeds in his breast pocket. He spits shells into the gutter, then sees Etta and stops.

@ETTA
If you're here because you heard the radio, don't tell me you're sorry like you knew him.

@JACK
He fixed my window last winter. Wouldn't take money. Said I'd only spend it on a better window.

@ETTA
That sounds like him.

A coroner's van pulls away. Bug watches it from the doorway, recorder hugged against his chest. [[CC: A van engine pulls away.]]

@JACK
Can I do anything?

@ETTA
Can you keep people from saying they knew what happened?

Jack has no answer. Etta goes inside. A shell is stuck to his shoe; he scrapes it off on the step.

INT. MORGUE CORRIDOR - FRIDAY MORNING

Etta stands at a counter. Jack has stayed behind the line she gave him. A CLERK slides over a list of personal effects: keys, laundry receipt, one empty glassine bag. Cause of death pending.

@ETTA
The man downstairs said overdose.

@CLERK
He isn't the medical examiner.

@JACK
Where's the bag?

@CLERK
With the property officer. You can request it when the report is filed.

Etta signs the release for Peanut's clothes. Jack notices the laundry receipt has a navy-yard delivery address and a penciled number: 13.

@ETTA
He was doing extra deliveries for the funeral fund at church. He said it paid cash.

@JACK
He say whose laundry?

@ETTA
No. He said it was laundry.

Jack copies the address onto the back of his seed wrapper. He asks before taking the receipt; Etta gives it to him.

INT. JACK'S OFFICE - FRIDAY LATE MORNING

A one-room office over a closed travel agency. The window Peanut repaired still sticks. Jack forces it, then finds a glassine bag on his desk with a note beneath it: WE KNOW WHAT YOU WERE.

LT. FRANK PIKE, 50s, stands by the filing cabinet, coat open. He has a policeman's patience for other people's things.

@PIKE
Your door was open.

@JACK
You opened it.

@PIKE
The Ray boy was carrying that brand. Sweet Jesus. Everybody's carrying it this week.

@JACK
You took evidence out of the property room to tell me that?

@PIKE
That's not evidence. That's a reminder.

Jack does not touch the bag. Pike watches the effort it costs him.

@PIKE
You used to know which door to kick and which one to leave alone. Then you needed what was behind them more than the badge.

@JACK
I haven't worn a badge in six years.

@PIKE
I know. I signed the paper.

Pike closes the window on his way out. Jack waits until he's gone, then wraps the glassine in the note and puts it in a drawer. [[CC: The office door closes.]]

INT. VETRANO'S COFFEE SHOP - FRIDAY LATE MORNING

Espresso cups rattle beneath framed photos of men shaking hands. CORNELIUS "CORN" BRANDT, 60s, sits alone with a ledger page turned face down. SAL VETRANO, his employer, watches from the counter.

Jack sits opposite. Corn pushes an envelope across without greeting him. Jack leaves it where it is.

@CORN
A woman who works for me mislaid a book. Numbers, names, receipts. I want the book, not the woman.

@JACK
Then ask her for it.

@CORN
I did. She said she didn't have it. Now I want an answer from somebody I don't have to pay all year.

Corn turns over the page. One line reads FULTON LAUNDRY. Beneath it, a date and a delivery to Warehouse 3.

@JACK
The Ray boy delivered this?

@CORN
Towels. Our people were using the laundry. The boy saw a cart and asked a question. People ask questions. That isn't a crime.

@JACK
Which question?

@CORN
If I knew, I'd have my book.

Corn opens the envelope. Cash. Five hundred dollars.

@CORN
You find it by eight tonight. That's the advance. Another fifteen hundred when it's in my hand.

@JACK
Why me?

@CORN
Because you knew the boy, and because a policeman won't take your call. You can get near people who won't talk to mine.

Jack looks at Sal. Sal looks away. Jack closes the envelope.

@JACK
If somebody hurt Peanut, the book isn't the first thing I bring back.

@CORN
Bring me the book first. The rest is your time.

Jack gets up. Corn stops him with one finger on the envelope.

@CORN
You tell the woman I asked nicely. Her name is Vera Voss.

Jack takes the money. He leaves sunflower shells on Corn's clean saucer. Corn stares at them until Sal calls him over.

INT. RAY APARTMENT - FRIDAY NOON

Etta is measuring Peanut's good suit against the bed. The trousers are too short; she lets out the hem with a seam ripper. Jack puts Corn's envelope on the dresser.

@ETTA
Where'd that come from?

@JACK
A man who wants me to find something. It isn't a loan.

@ETTA
Men don't give away five hundred dollars. Especially to you.

@JACK
He gave it to me. I'm giving it to you. Use it for Peanut.

Etta opens the envelope and counts it, because she has learned to count what men say is enough.

@ETTA
Does he want you looking at my son?

@JACK
He wants a book. I think the book and Peanut met each other.

@ETTA
And when you find it?

@JACK
I don't know yet.

@ETTA
That's the first honest thing anybody's brought me today.

She puts half the cash in a biscuit tin and the rest beside a funeral-home bill.

@ETTA
I'll take the money. I won't sell you his name. If the man asks what I bought, tell him shoes.

Jack nods. She goes back to the hem. Only after he has left does she put her face against the suit. [[CC: A door closes softly.]]

INT. SUNNY'S WASH-N-DRY - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

Bug sits under the folding table with the shoebox recorder. He has patched one headphone cup with black electrical tape. Jack crouches beside him, keeping his distance.

@BUG
Mom says you're looking for a book. Why would Peanut have a book?

@JACK
I'm trying to find that out.

Bug points to the penciled thirteen on the receipt.

@BUG
He brought the towels back with that. Said the men at the yard had a big machine that cleans ice. He wanted to ride it.

@JACK
He tell you anything else?

@BUG
He said one of the men didn't like him seeing what was inside the cart. He told me not to tell Mom because she already thought the job was stupid.

Jack points to the recorder.

@JACK
What's on there?

@BUG
The washer. And a man talking to Peanut on the step. I didn't hear him till this morning.

Bug holds the cassette, reluctant to put it in. Jack does not reach for it.

@JACK
You don't have to play it for me.

@BUG
I know. I just don't want him to stop being alive when it ends.

Bug plays the first few seconds: CLANK. CLANK. CLANK. Then stops it before the voices. [[CC: A washer clanks on tape; playback stops.]]

@JACK
Keep the tape. Don't give the only one to anybody.

INT. CITY PROPERTY OFFICE - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

A rubber stamp lands on a stack of transfer slips. WHITNEY KELLERMAN, 50s, deputy commissioner of city property, checks his cuff for ink. A wood-handled stamp the size of a coffee can waits beside his ledger. [[CC: A heavy stamp thuds against paper.]]

Pike stands over him. Through the glass, NICKY, a clerk with a rolling cart, sorts receipts in precise piles.

@KELLERMAN
The auction's public until seven forty-five. After that, the winning bidders sign for removal. There are witnesses on the floor.

@PIKE
Then they'll witness an auction.

Kellerman shows him a form for Lot Thirteen: INDUSTRIAL CLEANING COMPOUND, THIRTY KILOS. Two signatures and the city's seal.

@KELLERMAN
The manifest says cleaning compound. The warehouse book says otherwise. I don't like having two books.

@PIKE
Then make sure Corn takes his home.

@KELLERMAN
You told me the boy had a bag. You didn't tell me there'd be a mother.

@PIKE
There generally is.

Pike takes the transfer slips. Kellerman's ink-stained hand hovers over the telephone, then returns to the stamp.

INT. CHURCH KITCHEN - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

A line of children waits for sandwiches. HARRIETT, 30s, in a dark coat, checks a list while DOVE, 20s, cuts bread with a blunt knife. No one stops the children from being noisy. [[CC: Children chatter; a knife taps the board.]]

A volunteer answers the wall telephone and passes Harriett a penciled note. Harriett reads it twice: LOT 13, WAREHOUSE 3, EIGHT O'CLOCK.

@DOVE
Another donation?

@HARRIETT
Thirty kilos. Somebody wants us to keep it off the street.

@DOVE
Somebody wants us somewhere at eight.

@HARRIETT
Both can be true.

A little girl in a Dorothy costume asks Dove for the heel of the bread. He gives it to her.

@DOVE
Who called?

@HARRIETT
Didn't give a name. Knew which entrance the carts use. Said the public would be gone and the evidence men would have cleared out by eight.

@DOVE
And if they haven't?

@HARRIETT
Then we'll decide what we can do when we get there. Nobody shoots to protect a bag of powder.

She folds the note into her pocket. Before anything else, she finishes the sandwiches.

INT. VETRANO'S BACK OFFICE - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

VERA VOSS, late 30s, sharp and tired of being underestimated, balances Corn's books at a desk. Corn sits opposite with the page he showed Jack.

@CORN
Six years. I can't remember a single figure you forgot.

@VERA
I forgot your birthday twice.

@CORN
You never forgot the money.

He indicates a missing book on her desk. She keeps writing in another.

@VERA
I kept your accounts. You moved the book to the yard yourself.

@CORN
Because Pike was looking at it. Now somebody else is. There's a difference.

@VERA
Then send somebody else to pick it up.

Corn closes her pen in the ledger. The nib bends.

@CORN
I sent Jack Monroe. He finds things. You don't need to help him.

@VERA
You wouldn't have told me that if you wanted me to stay home.

@CORN
I need somebody to read what Kellerman puts in front of me. Eight o'clock. Warehouse Three. Bring a pen that works.

He leaves. Vera examines the bent nib, then opens a drawer. Inside is a city seal on a duplicate transfer slip. She slips it into her coat.

INT. NAVY YARD AUCTION OFFICE - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

Nicky checks the serial number on a pinball machine against his list. Jack stands at the window, examining the public auction catalog. A hand-lettered sign announces FINAL COLLECTION: MONDAY.

@JACK
Lot Thirteen. Cleaning compound?

@NICKY
Seized industrial goods. Sold as a sealed lot.

@JACK
Who buys cleaning compound by the kilo?

@NICKY
I'm recording the sale, not recommending it.

Jack slides the laundry receipt through the window. Nicky recognizes the handwriting before he can hide it.

@JACK
A kid delivered towels here yesterday. You signed for them.

@NICKY
He brought two carts. One to the office, one to Warehouse Three.

@JACK
What did he see?

@NICKY
A cart with a seal. Mr. Kellerman's seal. He asked why it had a police tag under the city tag.

@JACK
What'd you tell him?

@NICKY
I told him not to remove the top tag.

Jack waits. Nicky squares the corners of the receipt with the counter.

@NICKY
Mr. Lane was there. He took the boy outside. I logged the towels, not the conversation.

Nicky gives the receipt back. At the far end of the room, Kellerman enters with the oversized stamp. Nicky lowers his eyes and resumes his list.

EXT. NAVY YARD GATE - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

Jack passes a line of auction buyers. Among them, CLUTCH, a big Queens biker with a soft face, argues with a guard about a loading permit. A Spirit of '76 flyer is tucked into his jacket.

@CLUTCH
I paid for the whole machine. What do you mean the legs are separate?

@GUARD
I said you have to take the legs off to get it in your van.

@CLUTCH
Then say that. You make it sound like I got cheated.

Across the street, Vera watches Jack from a parked car. When he catches her eye, she drives away. He notes her license plate on the seed wrapper.

INT. SUNNY'S WASH-N-DRY - FRIDAY AFTERNOON

Bug and Jack fish in the washer's coin box with a bent coat hanger. Peanut had shown Bug a way to open it when the owner was late with change. [[CC: Metal scrapes and coins rattle.]]

A folded corner of paper comes out with three quarters. It is the bottom of a carbon transfer slip: LOT 13, THIRTY KILOS, and half of Kellerman's seal. On the back, Peanut's handwriting: TWO LABELS. ASK NICKY.

@BUG
He hid my birthday money in there once. Mom found it in a week.

@JACK
Why didn't you say anything about the box?

@BUG
I didn't know there was anything in it. You didn't ask.

Jack takes that. He puts the paper in an envelope, writes COPY FIRST on it, and hands it to Bug.

@JACK
Show your mother. Make two copies. Keep one somewhere that isn't here.

@BUG
On what? The copier at school costs a nickel.

Jack gives him a quarter. Bug holds it out for more. Jack gives him another.

INT. POOL HALL - FRIDAY LATE AFTERNOON

TICK LANE, 40s, in a dark coat and leather gloves, plays alone. A cigarette burns in an ashtray. Jack enters with the laundry receipt. [[CC: Pool balls crack together.]]

@JACK
Nicky says you were at the yard with Peanut Ray.

@TICK
A lot of people were at the yard. They're having an auction.

@JACK
He asked about a police tag. You took him outside.

@TICK
I told him where to put his cart.

Tick pockets a ball. He keeps his gloved hand on the cue.

@JACK
He turned up dead with your merchandise.

@TICK
You know what my merchandise is?

@JACK
I'm asking.

@TICK
You ought to be careful asking about it. People might remember you.

Jack spits shells into an empty ashtray. Tick watches him, irritated.

@JACK
Somebody already put a bag on my desk. Give Pike my thanks.

Tick's face moves a fraction. He covers it by taking his shot.

@TICK
You want to know where I was last night, ask Lieutenant Pike.

@JACK
I intend to.

Jack leaves. Tick misses an easy ball. He takes off one glove only after Jack is out the door; his hand stays below the edge of the table.

INT. PIKE'S CAR - FRIDAY LATE AFTERNOON

Pike's car is parked beneath the elevated tracks. Jack sits in the passenger seat, leaving the door open. Trains drown out half of what they say. [[CC: An elevated train rattles overhead.]]

@PIKE
Tick sells a little. The boy bought a little. The city writes it down and we all go home.

@JACK
You were very fast with that conclusion.

@PIKE
You were very fast taking Corn's money.

@JACK
I gave it away.

@PIKE
Doesn't change whose pocket it came from.

Pike produces a photograph of Jack outside the Ray apartment. Bug is in the doorway behind him.

@PIKE
The younger one shouldn't be talking to strangers. A lot of traffic on that street.

Jack's hand finds the door handle. He does not get out.

@JACK
You told me a building was empty once. I gave you the address. A woman was inside when your men came through the door.

@PIKE
You told yourself it was empty. You were looking for money by then.

@JACK
I was. That doesn't make her less dead.

For the first time, Pike has nothing prepared. Jack takes the photograph and gets out.

@PIKE
You can't put your old badge back on, Jack.

@JACK
I'm not asking for it.

INT. RAY APARTMENT - FRIDAY EARLY EVENING

Etta listens to the cassette with Bug and Jack. The room is still except for the tape spinning. The man's voice is indistinct beneath the washer; Peanut is not. [[CC: A washer clanks on tape.]]

@MAN (ON TAPE)
This one's on the house.

@PEANUT (ON TAPE)
What, you hit the lottery?

@MAN (ON TAPE)
Don't push it.

Peanut laughs. Etta reaches for STOP and misses it the first time. [[CC: Peanut laughs; the tape stops.]]

@ETTA
That's the last time?

@BUG
The last time I recorded him.

@ETTA
You hear the difference?

Bug nods. Jack puts Pike's photograph face down on the table.

@JACK
Tick Lane was there at the yard. Pike knows him. Neither of them wants the book to come out.

@ETTA
Do you know that voice?

@JACK
Not well enough to swear to it.

@ETTA
Then don't swear. Find something you can put beside it.

Bug hands her the copied transfer slip. She reads it, puts it beside the cassette, and writes the date across the top.

@ETTA
I want the book, too. Not for Corn. For every person who's going to tell me my son did this to himself and shut the door.

INT. DINER - FRIDAY EARLY EVENING

Vera sits in a booth with a cup of coffee and her coat still on. Jack slides in opposite. A waitress brings him a plate for his shells. He uses it. [[CC: Dishes clatter in a diner.]]

@VERA
Corn said you'd find me. He didn't say you'd take this long.

@JACK
You knew Peanut Ray?

@VERA
He brought laundry. He saw two labels on the cart and asked a clerk about them. Pike's friend took him outside.

@JACK
Tick Lane.

@VERA
I didn't see the man close enough. I saw Pike watching from the office.

@JACK
Where's Corn's book?

@VERA
On Nicky's cart after the auction. Corn insists on checking the buyer's receipt against his own ledger before anything leaves. He trusts numbers more than people.

@JACK
What are the numbers?

@VERA
Thirty kilos seized in a raid. Pike wrote up twenty. Kellerman stamped the rest as cleaning compound. Sal's people buy it back from the city for the price of a damaged forklift.

@JACK
And you wrote it down.

@VERA
Yes. With dates. With my name on the pages I prepared.

@JACK
Why didn't you take it before?

@VERA
Corn keeps it close. And a loose page gets called a forgery. The book has the ink, the dates, the stamps. All of them together might live longer than I will.

She gives him the duplicate transfer slip. Its seal matches Peanut's torn corner.

@VERA
I want the book out of that room. You want to know what happened to the boy. We can help each other until we can't.

@JACK
You have a car?

@VERA
You planning to walk there?

EXT. CHURCH YARD - FRIDAY EARLY EVENING

Harriett and Dove load blankets and an empty hand truck into a battered 1966 Cadillac hearse. Four other Panthers check the vehicle. A clipboard rests on the dashboard.

@DOVE
We don't have to take the guns inside.

@HARRIETT
No. But Pike's men know whose neighborhood that load is meant for. I don't want anybody surprised if they come armed.

A volunteer brings the phone note. Harriett makes the others read it.

@HARRIETT
The caller says the room's clear at eight. If that's wrong, we pull back and find another way. No heroics for the clipboard.

@DOVE
What about the powder?

@HARRIETT
If we get it, it doesn't go to anyone to sell. We count it, seal it, and get rid of it together.

The children march past in homemade Halloween costumes. Dove lets Dorothy climb into the empty back of the hearse, then helps her out before they leave.

INT. VERA'S CAR - FRIDAY EVENING

The car idles at a red light. Jack turns over Vera's duplicate slip and finds a telephone number written in her handwriting. Beside it: HARRIETT, 8:00.

@JACK
Whose number?

@VERA
A woman who can take Lot Thirteen somewhere Pike can't sell it.

@JACK
You sent her to the yard.

@VERA
I told her where it was.

@JACK
You told her eight. What did you tell her about the men who stay after the auction?

Vera keeps her eyes on the light. It turns green. She doesn't move. [[CC: A car horn sounds behind them.]]

@VERA
I said the room would be clear.

@JACK
It won't be. Pike has evidence men. Corn has his people. You told her a lie so they'd come through the door and everybody would turn around.

@VERA
While everybody turns around, I get the book.

@JACK
And she gets shot for it.

@VERA
Maybe. If I go in alone, I disappear with it. If I don't go in, those thirty kilos come back here in little bags. I made a choice.

@JACK
You made hers for her.

The horn sounds again. Vera pulls into a space, hard. Jack catches himself against the dashboard. [[CC: Tires scrape the curb.]]

@VERA
You took Corn's money.

@JACK
Yes. I gave it to Etta. That doesn't make it clean.

@VERA
You think you can take a book from Corn and leave everybody else untouched?

@JACK
No. I think you tell her what she's walking into, and she gets to choose.

Vera reaches for the note. Jack holds it for a moment, then gives it back. She gets out and runs for a payphone. He follows.

EXT. PAYPHONE - FRIDAY EVENING

Vera feeds coins into the payphone. Jack stands close enough to hear the ring, not close enough to take the receiver. [[CC: Coins drop; a telephone rings.]]

A VOLUNTEER answers at the church kitchen.

@VERA
Harriett, please. Tell her the evidence men will still be there. Pike will be there. The caller lied about the room being clear.

@VOLUNTEER (ON PHONE)
They left five minutes ago.

@VERA
Where are they coming in?

@VOLUNTEER (ON PHONE)
I don't know. Who is this?

@VERA
The person who called before.

The volunteer hangs up. Vera replaces the receiver. Jack takes the number from her and puts it in his pocket.

@JACK
When we get there, we tell them ourselves.

@VERA
If they get there.

@JACK
You don't get to hope they don't.

INT. BUG'S BEDROOM - FRIDAY EVENING

Two shoebox recorders face each other on the floor, joined by a cable. Bug makes a copy of DRYER BEAT while Etta writes names and numbers on envelopes. [[CC: Cassette motors whir.]]

@BUG
If you bring the whole cassette in there, they'll take it. Or break it.

@JACK
Your mother's keeping the copy.

@BUG
I'm making another thing.

Bug opens an old cassette shell. He cuts a short length of copied tape with a razor, joins the ends with splicing tape, and threads the little loop through the shell. It catches. He adjusts it with a pencil until it turns. Then he lets the second recorder dub the loop over and over onto a normal cassette while they prepare to leave.

@BUG
The little loop won't rewind, but this copy will. It'll repeat for a long time. The batteries are old, though.

The loop plays: the man's offer, Peanut's laugh, a rough splice, and the offer again. [[CC: A taped voice and a laugh repeat over a clank.]]

Jack cannot listen a second time. He presses STOP. Bug marks the repeated recording with a grease pencil and hands it over. Etta keeps the uncut original and an ordinary, uninterrupted copy.

@ETTA
If you find that book, don't bring it to the man who paid you.

@JACK
I won't.

@ETTA
Don't bring it to a policeman by himself, either. Bring it to me. I can still read.

@BUG
Can I come?

@ETTA
No.

@BUG
I can keep the machine running.

@ETTA
You can keep it running here.

Bug looks at Jack for support. Jack shakes his head. Bug turns the pencil in the cassette hub until the tape is taut.

INT. BROOKLYN NAVY YARD - WAREHOUSE 3 - FRIDAY NIGHT

The public auction is over. Chairs stack against a wall. Sodium light picks out a seized Cadillac, forty pinball machines glowing like a funeral arcade, a Zamboni, and the Copa mirror ball. [[CC: Pinball machines hum and chime.]]

SAL VETRANO'S men drink espresso by the bay doors. Pike's evidence men wear windbreakers and carry sidearms. Nicky pushes his cart through the room, recording each cleared lot.

Clutch stands beside his newly purchased Bally SPIRIT OF '76 on a dolly. He has nowhere to put it until his cousin brings a van on Monday.

@CLUTCH
I'm not leaving a machine I paid for in a room full of guys who sell machines.

@NICKY
Final collection is Monday. The receipt says so.

@CLUTCH
Then I'll wait till Monday.

@NICKY
I'll record that.

At a folding table, Kellerman writes in a receipt book. The big wood-handled stamp sits beside it. A sealed cart marked LOT 13 waits near the bay.

Vera enters carrying coffee. Jack stays in the dark between the Cadillacs. Corn takes a cup from her and nods toward Nicky's cart.

@CORN
Bring me the book. I want the numbers before I sign.

@VERA
You remember my pen is broken?

@CORN
Use Kellerman's.

Vera sets the coffee down. Jack sees Pike near the sealed cart. Tick stands behind him, one hand inside his coat. The clock over the bay reads eight-oh-nine.

Vera lifts the ledger from Nicky's cart with a coffee pot and a smile. Turns -- Corn has her wrist. Gentle. Terrible.

@CORN
Six years you kept my books.

@VERA
Six years I kept count.

@JACK (O.S.)
Let her go, Corn.

Jack comes out from between the Cadillacs, a .38 in a hand that shakes when he doesn't want it to. He spits a mouthful of shells on the concrete.

The shape of the room: Jack on Pike. Pike on Vera. Vera on Corn. Corn on Jack. A slow circle you could put on a dollar.

@PIKE
Put the gun down. You're outnumbered.

@JACK
You said the boy bought a bag. Let's ask the man who gave it to him.

Clutch wheels the Spirit of '76 straight through the middle of the circle.

@CLUTCH
Coming through. Coming through.

Every barrel tracks him because he's the only thing moving. He parks the machine beside a pillar and puts both hands up.

The bay doors come off the rails. A '66 Cadillac hearse backs in and stalls in exhaust. Six Panthers get out with guns and a clipboard. Eleven minutes late. [[CC: Metal crashes; an engine sputters and dies.]]

@HARRIETT
We're here for Lot Thirteen.

@NICKY
(writing)
Lot Thirteen. New bidder. Cash.

He keeps writing. He will keep writing for the rest of his life, which is four minutes.

@DOVE
There was a parade.

@HARRIETT
There was a schedule.

@DOVE
It was children. What you want, we run over Dorothy?

Guns swing to the hearse. Swing back. Nobody knows who they're pointing at and everybody's right. [[HOLD: 15]]

Jack looks at Harriett. He has one chance to tell her what Vera hid.

@JACK
Pike's men aren't leaving. She told you the room would be clear. She lied.

Harriett looks at Vera, who cannot meet her eyes. Dove shifts toward the cart. Harriett holds him back.

@HARRIETT
Nobody move for that lot until I say.

Jack pulls out Bug's shoebox recorder and sets it on the hand truck. He presses REWIND on the cassette of repeated copies. [[CC: A cassette rewinds.]] [[HOLD: 25]]

@JACK
It's rewinding. Nobody shoot. It's rewinding.

Fourteen guns listen to a tape rewind. The little counter turns; nobody puts a gun away. [[CC: The cassette keeps rewinding.]] [[HOLD: 20]]

Clutch slowly takes off his cap. Nicky writes down the arrival time, then checks it against the warehouse clock. [[CC: The tape reels continue to whir.]] [[HOLD: 20]]

Tick watches the recorder, then watches Jack. The counter is almost back to the start. No one has relaxed. [[CC: Rewinding continues.]] [[HOLD: 25]]

Squeal. Click. Jack hits PLAY. Dryers. CLANK. CLANK. [[CC: Tape squeals, clicks, and washer clanks play.]]

@MAN (ON TAPE)
This one's on the house.

@PEANUT (ON TAPE)
What, you hit the lottery?

Peanut laughs. Tick's hand goes to his pocket. Gold pinky ring. The same ring. [[CC: Peanut laughs on tape.]]

@JACK
You gave him the bag.

@TICK
I didn't tell him to take it.

Tick shoots Jack through the thigh and lunges for the recorder. Jack goes down hard. Blood straight through his pants. He tries to stand; the leg folds. He will not walk again in this film. [[CC: A gunshot cracks; Jack falls.]]

Pike shoots twice -- not cool, panicked -- because Tick is turning, mouth open.

@TICK
Pike told me to --

BANG. BANG. Gut. Tick goes down on his side, the recorder three feet away, still playing his voice and Peanut laughing. [[CC: Two gunshots crack.]]

The room opens fire. An evidence man goes backward through a pinball machine. Backglass strobes. Bells won't stop. He crawls behind it, alive. [[CC: Gunfire erupts; glass shatters and bells ring.]]

Kellerman goes under the table and pulls the cloth down. A flying splinter cuts his hand. We see his shoes. He stays there, clutching the stamp. [[CC: Gunshots and pinball bells continue.]]

Corn steps over Jack to finish him. Jack's revolver clicks on a bad round. His shaking fingers cannot work the jammed cylinder. [[CC: A revolver clicks.]]

Vera shoots Corn through the chest. He sits hard against the Cadillac. Not noble. Annoyed. One shoe came off. [[CC: A gunshot cracks.]]

@CORN
That's forty-one hundred, not forty-two -- you never could add -- somebody get my shoe --

He dies looking for the shoe. Vera drops beside Jack, still holding the ledger.

Nicky crouches behind his cart, pen moving.

@NICKY
Lot Thirteen -- discharge -- multiple -- unknown --

A stray round takes the side of his head. The pen keeps moving for a second after. Then stops. [[CC: A shot cracks; the pen scrapes the page.]]

Clutch takes one through the stomach trying to drag his machine down as cover. He ends up hugging its leg, crying.

@CLUTCH
Ma -- Ma -- oh Jesus Ma --

The machine TILTS, then lights up. PLAYER ONE. HIGH SCORE. Clutch's grip loosens. No one can reach him before he dies. [[CC: Pinball bells ring.]]

Tick is gut-shot, leaving a wide smear as he crawls for the recorder. An inch at a time. The repeated tape keeps playing. The fight moves away from him; nobody is coming. [[CC: The taped offer and laughter repeat.]] [[HOLD: 18]]

He hooks a finger under the handle. It slips. He tries to pull himself forward and has to stop, breathing in little pieces. The recorder is still just beyond him. [[CC: Peanut laughs on tape again.]] [[HOLD: 16]]

He gets his fingers to the plastic. He cannot lift it. He cannot make it stop. He lies there with his hand against it, gathering strength for one more try. [[CC: The taped offer repeats.]] [[HOLD: 18]]

He reaches again, slips, waits for a breath that doesn't come, and dies with his hand against the recorder. The laugh is still playing. [[CC: Peanut's laugh repeats on tape.]] [[HOLD: 16]]

The laugh plays on until the batteries sag, warp, and die. [[CC: The tape slows and warps to silence.]] [[HOLD: 8]]

Then music comes up. It replaces the boy. [[CC: Music rises.]]

Dove takes one in the shoulder. Two Panthers drag him to the hearse. Harriett grabs the hand truck with the dope and looks straight at Vera. She knows who sent the tip. She knows the clock was rigged. [[CC: A gunshot cracks; Dove cries out.]]

The Panthers back the hearse through the smoke with Dove and the sealed cart. Harriett counts the people in the vehicle before it moves. [[CC: An engine starts; metal scrapes.]]

Jack lies on his back, white, his belt tourniquet already soaked. Vera tucks the ledger inside his coat, rips a cord from a display lamp, and ties off above the belt with her teeth.

@JACK
You got the book?

@VERA
I got you. Shut up.

She drags him by the coat. He screams once, short. A heel-track in blood follows them all the way to the door. [[CC: Jack cries out.]]

Pike slips away in the smoke, scraping his forearm on the broken bay door. Blood runs into his cuff. Under the table: Kellerman's shoes, and his bleeding hand holding the stamp like a teddy bear.

INT. NEIGHBORHOOD CLINIC - FRIDAY NIGHT

A NURSE cuts Jack's trouser leg open. Vera's hands are stained to the wrist. The ledger sits in a brown grocery bag beneath her chair. [[CC: Scissors cut fabric; Jack breathes raggedly.]]

@NURSE
How long has that cord been on?

@VERA
I don't know. We had to get him out.

@NURSE
I'm not blaming you. I need the time.

@JACK
The clock said eight-eleven when they came in. A few minutes after that.

The nurse works. Jack grips the edge of the table until his knuckles whiten.

@NURSE
The hospital has a surgeon. You're going there now, and you're staying off that leg.

@JACK
Can somebody call Etta Ray?

Vera reaches into Jack's coat, finds Etta's number, and passes it to the nurse. She does not take the ledger away.

@VERA
Corn won't be asking for the book.

@JACK
His people will.

@VERA
Then we'd better make sure it isn't the only one.

INT. CITY PROPERTY OFFICE - SATURDAY MORNING

Kellerman's hand is bandaged. The oversized stamp sits in a clear evidence bag on his desk. The surviving evidence man gives a statement to two investigators in the next room.

Pike, his forearm dressed, arrives to collect his coat. An INVESTIGATOR blocks the doorway.

@INVESTIGATOR
You don't get to take anything off this floor until we inventory it.

@PIKE
I'm a lieutenant.

@INVESTIGATOR
I can read your badge. Leave it on the desk.

Pike sets down the badge. It makes a small sound. Kellerman studies the evidence bag, then signs a notice requiring him to appear for questioning. He does not look at Pike. [[CC: A badge taps the desk.]]

INT. RAY APARTMENT - SUNDAY AFTERNOON

Jack sits in a borrowed wheelchair, leg heavily dressed and elevated on a chair. He is pale and angry at every movement. Etta changes the cloth beneath his heel while he dictates from Corn's ledger.

Bug works the two recorders: one plays the original tape, the other makes another ordinary copy. Tape spaghetti covers the floor. Vera sits at the table with carbon paper, copying ledger pages by hand. [[CC: Cassette motors whir; paper rustles.]]

@JACK
Page forty-one. Kellerman's stamp. Pike's badge number. Twenty kilos on the seizure report, thirty on the sale.

@VERA
Thirty on the sale plus twenty reported. Fifty in the original raid.

@JACK
Read it back.

She does. Etta marks the page number and initials each copy.

@BUG
This wire's bad. If I hold it here, it records. If I let go, it's just humming.

@ETTA
Then I'll hold it while you eat.

@BUG
I can eat with the other hand.

@ETTA
You can also let somebody help you.

He gives her the wire. She holds it exactly where he showed her. Jack notices Vera copying a page with her own signature.

@JACK
You can leave that one out.

@VERA
No. I wrote it.

@ETTA
Good. The reporter gets a set. My lawyer gets a set. The church gets a set. Nobody gets to be the only person holding it.

@JACK
Pike will say I made it up.

@ETTA
He's welcome to come say it where everybody can hear him.

INT. SUNNY'S WASH-N-DRY - MONDAY MORNING

Bug wheels Jack through the laundromat. The sixth washer still clanks. Jack's injured leg is supported; he does not stand. [[CC: The washer clanks.]]

Jack takes Pike's glassine and its note from his coat. He has carried them since Friday. Bug looks away, giving him privacy without leaving him alone.

Jack empties the bag into the deep utility sink and runs the tap until it is gone. The paper note follows into the wastebasket. [[CC: Water runs down a sink.]]

@BUG
You want me to wheel you back?

@JACK
In a minute.

They listen to the washer. Bug does not record it this time.

INT. CHURCH BASEMENT - THREE DAYS LATER - DAY

Folding chairs. No organ. Etta stands with the ledger, the copied pages, and a copy of the tape. A reporter and a lawyer sit among the neighbors. Jack is in the wheelchair by the wall. [[CC: Chairs scrape; a room settles.]]

Etta doesn't preach. She lays out a transfer slip beside Peanut's torn corner, the stamp impressions matching.

@ETTA
Tick Lane. Lieutenant Frank Pike. Whitney Kellerman.

@CONGREGATION
Amen.

In the back row Pike sits in civilian clothes, his forearm bandaged. His badge is gone. He came because if he didn't it would look worse. He has to hear his name said aloud in a room full of witnesses.

@ETTA
My son asked what was under a label. A man gave him a bag. After he died, another man told me what boys like him do. We have the paper and the tape. We're giving them to people who can ask the rest.

She plays the copy. Peanut laughs. Nobody rushes to turn it off. [[CC: A taped voice speaks; Peanut laughs.]]

A woman in the front row takes one set of pages. The reporter takes another. Etta gives the lawyer the original ledger. No verdict is promised; no one is asked to be quiet.

EXT. CHURCH STEPS - MONDAY DAY

Harriett waits for Vera. Dove's arm is in a sling behind her. The hearse is parked at the curb, its sealed load still inside.

@HARRIETT
You put us on a clock you knew was rigged. He got shot for your ledger.

Vera doesn't lie.

@VERA
I know.

@HARRIETT
You leave your pages in?

@VERA
I left them.

Harriett nods once. Not forgiveness. Accounting.

@HARRIETT
Then we're even. Don't call us again.

Dove climbs carefully into the hearse. Harriett checks the seal with him, then gets behind the wheel. Thirty kilos will be in the Hudson by dark. One river. That's the only river. [[CC: The hearse engine starts and drives away.]]

INT. BUG'S BEDROOM - MONDAY DAY

Razor blade, tape, grease pencil. Bug cuts the dryer CLANK from a copy and joins the ends. A period tape splice, not a sampler. He feeds the loop through a pencil and back into the recorder. [[CC: Tape snips; a cassette clicks.]]

The final track of the movie builds from his brother's last afternoon. First the clank. Then another clank. A beat made by hand. [[CC: The washer clank becomes a rhythm.]]

Bug reaches for another piece of tape. Before the beat drops: Peanut laughing. [[CC: Peanut laughs.]]

SMASH TO BLACK.
